<h2>4. Edge Client Architecture (React Native & Expo)</h2>
<p>
The edge tier operates as an autonomous, self-sufficient offline unit. The core mobile application is architected with clear boundaries between persistence, state synchronization, and map rendering:
</p>

<h3>4.1 State & Persistence Layer</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>expo-sqlite (Local Database)</strong>: Replicates a hardened local mirror of the server tables. Operates with write-ahead logging (WAL mode) enabled for rapid offline inserts.</li>
    <li><strong>Client Sync Queue</strong>: Every mutation (loss report submission, status update, photo capture) creates an immutable record in <code>local_sync_queue</code> with a unique <code>queue_id</code> and a client-side generated <code>client_report_id</code> (UUIDv4).</li>
    <li><strong>Zustand State Stores</strong>: Lightweight state containers manage active report drafts, network availability states, and downloaded mission packages in memory.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4.2 Offline Mapping Engine</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>MapLibre React Native</strong>: Avoids proprietary online-only map dependencies. Uses self-contained vector tile packages (MBTiles) bounded to the Chennai region (Lat: 12.90°N to 13.25°N, Lon: 80.10°E to 80.45°E).</li>
    <li><strong>GeoJSON Layer Rendering</strong>: Ingests the <code>drift_heatmap_geojson</code> and <code>planned_route_geojson</code> directly into MapLibre Source and Layer components without requiring web tile servers at sea.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4.3 Offline Synchronization Logic</h3>
<div class="callout">
<strong>Sync Guarantee:</strong> The client enforces <em>At-Least-Once Delivery with Server Idempotency</em>. Data loss is mitigated even if the mobile device crashes, loses battery, or undergoes intermittent network transitions.
</div>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Network Monitoring</strong>: <code>NetInfo.addEventListener</code> detects network reachability.</li>
    <li><strong>Batch Processing</strong>: Upon connectivity restoration, the sync manager queries all entries with <code>status = 'pending'</code> from <code>local_sync_queue</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Backoff & Jitter Policy</strong>: Failed transmissions trigger retry attempts according to $t_{\text{wait}} = 2^{\text{retry\_count}} + \text{rand}(0, 1)$ seconds, capped at 5 retries.</li>
    <li><strong>Conflict Resolution</strong>: Adopts Last-Write-Wins (LWW) with server-authoritative timestamps for updates, while new reports are completely additive and de-duplicated by <code>client_report_id</code>.</li>
</ol>

<h2>5. AI Drift Prediction & Spatial Risk Engine</h2>

<h3>5.1 Lagrangian Particle Tracking Formulation</h3>
<p>
The prediction engine models lost nets as an ensemble of $N = 5,000$ virtual particles initialized at the reported loss coordinate $(\lambda_0, \phi_0)$. Virtual particles account for observational uncertainty by sampling an initial Gaussian position perturbation ($\sigma = 50\text{ m}$).
</p>
<p>
The discrete-time trajectory for particle $i$ over hourly time steps $\Delta t = 3600\text{ s}$ up to $T = 72\text{ hours}$ is governed by:
</p>
<pre>x(t + dt) = x(t) + [ u_current + (alpha * u_wind) + (beta * u_wave) + eta ] * dt</pre>

<p>Where the components represent:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Ocean Current Velocity ($\vec{u}_{\text{current}}$)</strong>: Vector $(u, v)$ in $\text{m/s}$ fetched from Open-Meteo Marine API at surface depth.</li>
    <li><strong>Windage Factor ($\alpha$)</strong>: Drag coefficient calibrated by gear buoyancy:
        <ul>
            <li>Surface Buoys / Floats: $\alpha = 0.035 - 0.045$ (high surface exposure)</li>
            <li>Suspended Gillnets: $\alpha = 0.010 - 0.015$ (subsurface drag dominance)</li>
            <li>Demersal Trawls / Metal Traps: $\alpha = 0.002 - 0.005$ (near-bottom anchor effect)</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Stokes Wave Drift ($\beta \cdot \vec{u}_{\text{wave}}$)</strong>: Surface wave momentum calculated from significant wave height ($H_s$), wave direction ($\theta_w$), and peak period ($T_p$) ($\beta \approx 0.015$).</li>
    <li><strong>Stochastic Diffusion ($\vec{\eta}$)</strong>: Turbulent Brownian diffusion representing unresolved sub-grid eddies:
        <br><code>eta = Normal(0, sqrt(2 * Kh * dt))</code> where $K_h = 1.0\text{ m}^2/\text{s}$.
    </li>
</ul>

<h3>5.2 Probability Density Gridding</h3>
<p>
At intervals $t = 24\text{h}, 48\text{h}, 72\text{h}$, particle coordinates are binned into a regular geospatial matrix ($\Delta\lambda, \Delta\phi = 0.002^\circ \approx 220\text{ m}$). Cell density is computed as:
</p>
<pre>Density(i, j) = ParticleCount(i, j) / TotalParticles</pre>
<p>
Cells are vectorized into closed polygons using GeoPandas and contour algorithms, generating GeoJSON FeatureCollections categorized by probability levels.
</p>

<h3>5.3 Composite Spatial Risk Scoring Formula</h3>
<p>
Predicted drift zones are evaluated against critical coastal habitats and human navigation corridors off Chennai:
</p>
<pre>Composite Risk Score = (Density * 0.45) + (Ecological * 0.30) + (FishingActivity * 0.15) + (Navigation * 0.10)</pre>

<table>
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Zone Score</th>
            <th>Classification</th>
            <th>Recommended Action</th>
            <th>Chennai Regional Spatial Benchmark</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>76 – 100</strong></td>
            <td><span class="tag-critical">CRITICAL</span></td>
            <td>Immediate Recovery Alert</td>
            <td>Adyar Estuary mouth (13.00°N, 80.27°E) & Olive Ridley turtle breeding strip</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>51 – 75</strong></td>
